#: ../../source/moo_README.md:3 ../../source/moo_README.md:18
msgid "Moo!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:6
msgid ""
"Moo is a highly-optimised tokenizer/lexer generator. Use it to tokenize "
"your strings, before parsing 'em with a parser like "
"[nearley](https://github.com/hardmath123/nearley) or whatever else you're"
" into."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:8
msgid "[Fast](#is-it-fast)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:9
msgid "[Convenient](#usage)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:10
msgid "uses [Regular Expressions](#on-regular-expressions)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:11
msgid "tracks [Line Numbers](#line-numbers)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:12
msgid "handles [Keywords](#keywords)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:13
msgid "supports [States](#states)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:14
msgid "custom [Errors](#errors)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:15
msgid "is even [Iterable](#iteration)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:16
msgid "has no dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:17
msgid "4KB minified + gzipped"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:20
msgid "Is it fast?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:23
msgid "Yup! Flying-cows-and-singed-steak fast."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:25
msgid ""
"Moo is one of the fastest JS tokenizers around. When I benchmarked it in "
"2017 I concluded:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:27
msgid ""
"It's **~2–10x** faster than most other tokenizers; it's a **couple orders"
" of magnitude** faster than some of the slower ones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:29
msgid ""
"Define your tokens **using regular expressions**. Moo will compile 'em "
"down to a **single RegExp for performance**. It uses the new ES6 **sticky"
" flag** where possible to make things faster; otherwise it falls back to "
"an almost-as-efficient workaround. (For more than you ever wanted to know"
" about this, read [adventures in the land of substrings and "
"RegExps](http://mrale.ph/blog/2016/11/23/making-less-dart-faster.html).)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:31
msgid ""
"You _might_ be able to go faster still by writing your lexer by hand "
"rather than using RegExps, but that's icky."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:33
msgid ""
"Oh, and it [avoids parsing RegExps by itself](https://hackernoon.com/the-"
"madness-of-parsing-real-world-javascript-regexps-"
"d9ee336df983#.2l8qu3l76). Because that would be horrible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:36
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:39
msgid ""
"First, you need to do the needful: `$ npm install moo`, or whatever will "
"ship this code to your computer. Alternatively, grab the `moo.js` file by"
" itself and slap it into your web page via a `<script>` tag; moo is "
"completely standalone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:41
msgid "Then you can start roasting your very own lexer/tokenizer:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:58
msgid "And now throw some text at it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:69
msgid ""
"When you reach the end of Moo's internal buffer, next() will return "
"`undefined`. You can always `reset()` it and feed it more data when that "
"happens."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:72
msgid "On Regular Expressions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:75
msgid ""
"RegExps are nifty for making tokenizers, but they can be a bit of a pain."
" Here are some things to be aware of:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:77
msgid ""
"You often want to use **non-greedy quantifiers**: e.g. `*?` instead of "
"`*`. Otherwise your tokens will be longer than you expect:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:89
msgid "Better:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:103
msgid "The **order of your rules** matters. Earlier ones will take precedence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:117
msgid ""
"Moo uses **multiline RegExps**. This has a few quirks: for example, the "
"**dot `/./` doesn't include newlines**. Use `[^]` instead if you want to "
"match newlines too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:119
msgid ""
"Since an excluding character ranges like `/[^ ]/` (which matches anything"
" but a space) _will_ include newlines, you have to be careful not to "
"include them by accident! In particular, the whitespace metacharacter "
"`\\s` includes newlines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:122
msgid "Line Numbers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:125
msgid "Moo tracks detailed information about the input for you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:127
msgid ""
"It will track line numbers, as long as you **apply the `lineBreaks: true`"
" option to any rules which might contain newlines**. Moo will try to warn"
" you if you forget to do this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:129
msgid ""
"Note that this is `false` by default, for performance reasons: counting "
"the number of lines in a matched token has a small cost. For optimal "
"performance, only match newlines inside a dedicated token:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:136
msgid "Token Info"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:138
msgid "Token objects (returned from `next()`) have the following attributes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:140
msgid "**`type`**: the name of the group, as passed to compile."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:141
msgid "**`text`**: the string that was matched."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:142
msgid ""
"**`value`**: the string that was matched, transformed by your `value` "
"function (if any)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:143
msgid ""
"**`offset`**: the number of bytes from the start of the buffer where the "
"match starts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:144
msgid ""
"**`lineBreaks`**: the number of line breaks found in the match. (Always "
"zero if this rule has `lineBreaks: false`.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:145
msgid ""
"**`line`**: the line number of the beginning of the match, starting from "
"1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:146
msgid "**`col`**: the column where the match begins, starting from 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:149
msgid "Value vs. Text"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:151
msgid ""
"The `value` is the same as the `text`, unless you provide a [value "
"transform](#transform)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:166
msgid "Reset"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:168
msgid ""
"Calling `reset()` on your lexer will empty its internal buffer, and set "
"the line, column, and offset counts back to their initial value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:170
msgid ""
"If you don't want this, you can `save()` the state, and later pass it as "
"the second argument to `reset()` to explicitly control the internal state"
" of the lexer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:183
msgid "Keywords"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:186
msgid "Moo makes it convenient to define literals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:196
msgid ""
"It'll automatically compile them into regular expressions, escaping them "
"where necessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:198
msgid "**Keywords** should be written using the `keywords` transform."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:210
msgid "Why?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:212
msgid ""
"You need to do this to ensure the **longest match** principle applies, "
"even in edge cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:214
msgid "Imagine trying to parse the input `className` with the following rules:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:221
msgid ""
"You'll get _two_ tokens — `['class', 'Name']` -- which is _not_ what you "
"want! If you swap the order of the rules, you'll fix this example; but "
"now you'll lex `class` wrong (as an `identifier`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:223
msgid ""
"The keywords helper checks matches against the list of keywords; if any "
"of them match, it uses the type `'keyword'` instead of `'identifier'` "
"(for this example)."
msgstr ""

