msgid "You can use `Object.fromEntries` to easily construct keyword objects:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:252
msgid "States"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:255
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Moo allows you to define multiple lexer **states**. Each state defines "
"its own separate set of token rules. Your lexer will start off in the "
"first state given to `moo.states({})`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:257
msgid ""
"Rules can be annotated with `next`, `push`, and `pop`, to change the "
"current state after that token is matched. A \"stack\" of past states is "
"kept, which is used by `push` and `pop`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:259
msgid ""
"**`next: 'bar'`** moves to the state named `bar`. (The stack is not "
"changed.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:260
msgid ""
"**`push: 'bar'`** moves to the state named `bar`, and pushes the old "
"state onto the stack."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:261
msgid ""
"**`pop: 1`** removes one state from the top of the stack, and moves to "
"that state. (Only `1` is supported.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:263
msgid ""
"Only rules from the current state can be matched. You need to copy your "
"rule into all the states you want it to be matched in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:265
msgid ""
"For example, to tokenize JS-style string interpolation such as `a${{c: "
"d}}e`, you might use:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:288
msgid ""
"The `rbrace` rule is annotated with `pop`, so it moves from the `main` "
"state into either `lit` or `main`, depending on the stack."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:291
msgid "Errors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:294
msgid ""
"If none of your rules match, Moo will throw an Error; since it doesn't "
"know what else to do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:296
msgid ""
"If you prefer, you can have moo return an error token instead of throwing"
" an exception. The error token will contain the whole of the rest of the "
"buffer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:309
msgid ""
"You can have a token type that both matches tokens _and_ contains error "
"values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:318
msgid "Formatting errors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:320
msgid ""
"If you want to throw an error from your parser, you might find "
"`formatError` helpful. Call it with the offending token:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/moo_README.md:326
msgid "It returns a string with a pretty error message."
msgstr ""

